WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:08.099
Yeah, I grew up in a college town. It's specifically a college that's all

00:00:08.099 --> 00:00:14.280
about science and engineering. It was a Manhattan Project outpost. There

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:21.240
were, you know, kids in my Boy Scout Troop whose dads had worked refining

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:26.340
uranium for the Manhattan Project. And
when you're a kid, wherever you grow up,

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:31.439
you think is normal. Maybe you've had this experience. And you

00:00:31.439 --> 00:00:36.000
don't realize how weird it was until you
go other places and meet other

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:43.140
people. So I had a lot of a science and engineering mentality sort

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:51.390
of baked in from the very beginning. So I'll tell an anecdote that

00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:58.469
sums up Ames, Iowa in in my youth. Okay so in my Boy Scout troop, some of the dads

00:00:58.469 --> 00:01:02.550
came up with an idea. We did all the
normal Boy Scout things like Pinewood

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:07.110
Derby and whittling arrowheads and that
kind of stuff. But they came up with this

00:01:07.110 --> 00:01:15.810
idea which was that one of these guys
worked in the AG Engineering department

00:01:15.810 --> 00:01:21.930
he's the co-inventor of the cylindrical
hay bale. And he took some corn seeds

00:01:21.930 --> 00:01:26.790
that were as as close to being
genetically identical as he could get.

00:01:26.790 --> 00:01:32.100
They weren't cloned necessarily, but like
maybe all from the same head of corn or

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:34.560
something, ear of corn. And he carried them across

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:40.079
the campus to the office of another dad
who was one of these Manhattan Project

00:01:40.079 --> 00:01:44.759
guys. And that dad took it down to the
sub-basement of this building where the

00:01:44.759 --> 00:01:49.009
hot room was. So there's a like
three-foot thick wall of leaded glass

00:01:49.009 --> 00:01:55.530
with manipulator arms and on the other
side of it is incredibly radioactive

00:01:55.530 --> 00:02:00.540
stuff. And so that dad took the corn
seeds and put them next to really

00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:05.340
radioactive stuff for differing amounts
of time. And then painted them

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:10.560
different colors. The red
ones had been really exposed to a lot of

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:13.799
radiation and you know so on and so forth. And then

00:02:13.799 --> 00:02:20.189
those were handed out to us at the next
meeting of the Boy Scout troop.

00:02:20.189 --> 00:02:26.280
And the assignment was to plant them and cultivate these seeds and then after a

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:30.000
month, we bring the plants in and there'd
be a prize given out for the tallest,

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.230
healthiest corn plant, and another prize
for the weirdest mutation. And this is

00:02:37.230 --> 00:02:44.129
all completely deadpan, no hoopla, no
sense of how weird this was. That's like

00:02:44.129 --> 00:02:52.470
yes, one of the things you do in Boy
Scouts, grow mutant plants. So

00:02:52.470 --> 00:02:58.129
that's kind of where I came from.

00:02:58.159 --> 00:03:06.030
So I studied scientific type
topics in school but I always reserved

00:03:06.030 --> 00:03:11.280
some hope that I might be able to make a
living as a writer as well. And so I just

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:17.909
didn't study it because I figured I
could kind of work that on my own and so

00:03:17.909 --> 00:03:24.540
then after I graduated from college I
started writing books. And on the

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:29.250
third one I got very lucky, a whole bunch
of things randomly fell into place for

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:35.489
me. And I got an agent, I got an editor I
was able to publish a book and so so

00:03:35.489 --> 00:03:44.009
that's what I ended up doing. KEVIN KELLY: So you've never had a real job?

00:03:44.009 --> 00:03:51.359
STEPHENSON: Well, I've had real shitty jobs. lf you mean like a career kind of path job I've

00:03:51.359 --> 00:03:55.099
not really had one of those.

